PUBLIC RESPONSE: A. Bravehearts: Men in Skirts

Ellsworth develops the figure of pedagogy’s hinge as a spatial / performative threshold that stages the relation of inside and outside in order to reconfigure personal and social boundaries. In *Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Pedagogy* the hinge is illustrated with a set of museum, architectural and performative sites in which the passage between inside/outside, public/private, spectator/participant is made operative.

The sites she works through are, inter alia:

* The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, and in particular its Hall of Witness / entrance sequence, as an architectural pedagogy of witnessing that hinges memory and the public street;
* The Jane Addams Hull-House Museum, read through its porch, doorways and domestic/public rooms as a historic hinge of settlement work and community learning;
* The Children’s Museum of Manhattan’s Art Inside Out, the programme that literally moves art out of the gallery into the street and brings the street back in, a literal inside/outside hinge;
* Anna Deavere Smith’s verbatim performance work, notably *Fires in the Mirror* / *Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992*, as a performative hinge in which the performer/audience, self/other, story and event are made contiguous.

The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is the case that is not developed as a pedagogy’s-hinge example in the book, and *Bravehearts: Men in Skirts* is not used as an illustrative case in *Places of Learning*. The four cases most commonly cited with the hinge are the US Holocaust Museum, Hull-House Museum, CMOM Art Inside Out and Anna Deavere Smith’s performances.

