In *Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Agency in Postserial Space* Ellsworth develops pedagogy’s hinge as an architectural and curatorial threshold that stages a relation between inside and outside in order to re-work personal and social boundaries. The sites she returns to as hinge cases are museum / social-reform architectures and verbatim public performance that operate as architecturalized thresholds.

The hinge discussion is built around:

* The U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum, Washington, D.C., as an architectural narrative of entry, descent and remembrance that puts the visitor’s interiority in relation to a public history;
* The Jane Addams Hull-House Museum, Chicago, as a historic settlement house re-opened as a museum that re-activates the domestic/public boundary central to Addams’s social pedagogy;
* The Manhattan Children’s Museum’s Art Inside Out project, in which children’s work is made visible on the street façade, literally hinging interior making with exterior public circulation;
* Anna Deavere Smith’s verbatim performance work, e.g. *Fires in the Mirror* and *Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992*, which Ellsworth treats as a performative hinge between private testimony and public space;
* Bravehearts: Men in Skirts, the public intervention/ documentary gathering of men in kilts/skirts, which Ellsworth uses as an embodied, non-institutional example of a hinge that re-configures gendered inside/outside and private/public boundaries.

The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is not discussed in *Places of Learning* as a hinge or as a pedagogical case study in the book.

